--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/verification/aws-legacy-retirement-20261104/RESULTS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,30 @@
+# Post-cutover patch for review
+
+Private base: `2c4ac0c06afe9d5001c21f70bbd6bcd4a34329fb`. Apply this patch after the release owner verifies the actual AWS data and website. No changes to schema migrations 003–006, production resources, friend migration data, scheduled workflows, Git branches or deployments were performed in preparing it.
+
+The four JavaScript collection commands retain their provider request logic. Their new worker-only client requires the writer's INGEST_DATABASE_URL and scoped S3 credentials. The application accepts only DATABASE_URL and uses the read-only relational app. Readers cannot become writers by supplying a legacy URL. Production PostgreSQL connections require RDS and verify the CA certificate. The JS worker caches validated parsed inputs by source path and actual digest, caps individual cache entries, retries a changed version, and uses the original immutable version key when a mutable source path has moved. Only loopback fixture mode can override the S3 endpoint. Python staging uses the same explicit AWS writer configuration. Bootstrap exports these variables only for the writer role.
+
+The standalone collector's default now publishes normalized tables. It holds the same PostgreSQL advisory lease. A single scoped historical run selects only its league's oldest dirty markers, up to 20. Daily/pulse without a target take the latest marked partition for each historic priority league (MLS then Saudi) before at most 20 ordinary jobs. Existing GitHub collection commands retain --skip-materialize because their workflow already runs the relational publisher afterwards. The package daily/history aliases call this same standalone default. The ordinary rematerialize job's ordering and 003–006 schema files are unchanged. Scoped source discovery still records independent dirty jobs for a later global run. A scoped run never processes unrelated partitions/global catalog rows and never certifies unexamined global inputs as clean.
+
+Legacy web serving modules and three dedicated historical runtime verification commands were retired from the current source tree. AWS-only app.js is unconditional. The old '/data' delivery assertions are preserved in Git history and superseded by working AWS public API, byte/304 and immutable image alias E2Es. The default `verify` and `verify:aws` package commands execute the real disposable PostgreSQL materializer/API proof, with results in `dashboard/outputs/aws-runtime-verification/`. The application bootstrap test now starts the real built Next site against a distinct local API origin. Its competition-domain companion originally required two untracked raw files, which are absent in a clean checkout. It now renders from the checked-in published league evidence, retaining the Ligue 1, calendar-year and World Cup selector assertions.
+
+Remaining names are listed in inventory.json. File names such as neon_store.py and neon_ingest.py preserve import/CLI compatibility; they are no longer provider selectors. The shared competition_metadata and merge_sample routines remain in neon_materialize.py and are still used by the relational builder. The old object-publishing implementation there receives no calls from standard package/worker/standalone ingestion routes. Historical data key prefixes, the stored source formats, the already applied migration filename, collector User-Agent and workflow concurrency identity are unchanged. The old manual migration/Neon-specific audit utilities are outside current AWS entry points. The two old manual scripts that construct the current Store explicitly refuse AWS writer configuration; historical replays use the pinned original source with isolated historical credentials.
+
+## Reproduce
+
+From the private checkout, install dashboard dependencies with `cd dashboard && bun install --frozen-lockfile`. The worker E2E uses Node 26, Bun 1.4.4, embedded-postgres 18.6.0-beta.19, and Python with psycopg 3/boto3. Install the isolated S3 service with `npm install --prefix /tmp/statarena-js-worker-s3-20261104 --no-audit --no-fund --ignore-scripts s3rver@3.7.1`. The store is disk-backed with real requests by both AWS SDK clients to an ephemeral loopback port. It is not a live AWS bucket.
+
+```sh
+bun docs/verification/aws-legacy-retirement-20261104/verify.mjs after
+STATARENA_TEST_TOOLS=/tmp/statarena-archive-remat-pg STATARENA_TEST_PYTHON=/tmp/statarena-archive-remat-pg/python bun docs/verification/aws-actions-20261104/run.mjs retirement-staging
+STATARENA_TEST_TOOLS=/tmp/statarena-archive-remat-pg STATARENA_TEST_PYTHON=/tmp/statarena-archive-remat-pg/python bun docs/verification/aws-rematerialize-20261104/run.mjs retirement-queues
+cd dashboard && STATARENA_TEST_TOOLS=/tmp/statarena-archive-remat-pg STATARENA_TEST_PYTHONPATH=/tmp/statarena-archive-remat-pg/python bun run verify
+```
+
+Observed on localhost: actual worker/source parser and staged PostgreSQL/S3 workflow passed, including corruption rejection, historical version recovery, original ordinal/document ID references, stage writes, and rejection of writer/runtime alias confusion. The test ran the actual collector processes with deterministic provider responses and no live provider calls. It also invoked both package modes against the same local services. Targeted publication left four unrelated jobs; daily processed exactly two priority slots plus twenty ordinary jobs; pulse preserved the first two priorities; explicit skip left a real SQL dirty marker pending until the historical package alias processed it. The ordinary publisher's 16 original end-to-end workflows passed. AWS bootstrap/role harness passed 14/14, publisher core passed 5/5, AWS HTTP passed 10/10, and the Next/bootstrap/competition checks passed 3/3. Seven existing daily-refresh checks also passed.
+
+before.json records the missing worker client, python-before.json the missing Python writer configuration, and collector-before.json refusal of the old standalone default. Two fixture failures are retained: initially the targeted league had no finished fixture; then a genuinely staged orphan detail correctly appeared in the general queue. The corrected fixture uses finished scores and >20 unrelated jobs to exercise the real bound. No provider availability, real IAM permissions or deployed release is inferred from this isolated replay.
+
+Suggested commit `refactor: retire legacy web reads and isolate AWS staging writers`.
+
+A nested legacy observation envelope (`observation.response.data`) has been explicitly checked as well. The red receipt shows the formerly missing lineup data. The corrected worker preserves both the nested response and ordinary standalone references. The final worker fixture verifies a positive EURO score [1,0], a UEFA team mapping, a newly observed coach portrait, and a repaired club photo with its original Commons author and license. Unsupported fake provider URLs return 404 and do not overwrite previous source successes.
